  <header><div class="wrap"><a class="brand" href="/">JCF INFRA</a><nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/#projects">Projects</a><a href="/es/#projects" lang="es">Portafolio en español</a><a href="/#contact">Contact</a></nav></div></header>
  <main>
    <div class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">SECURITY LAB / 001</p><h1>Blocking Git metadata requests at the edge</h1><p class="lead">From a real request in Cloudflare Analytics to a focused WAF rule, with a clear distinction between observed evidence and unverified assumptions.</p><span class="badge">Cloudflare Free</span><span class="badge">Custom rule · Block</span><span class="badge">Case date · 14 October 2026</span></div></div>
    <div class="wrap content">
      <p class="notice"><strong>Outcome:</strong> a custom rule was configured and a subsequent browser test displayed a Cloudflare block page. This evidence does not establish that Git files were exposed, downloaded or used to compromise the site.</p>
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="Lab contents"><a href="#observation">Observation</a><a href="#control">Control</a><a href="#validation">Validation</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#limits">Limits</a></nav>
      <section id="observation"><h2>01 / Investigate the request</h2><p>Cloudflare sampled logs recorded a <code>GET /.git/config</code> request to <code>jcfinfra.com</code> at <strong>22:38:37 GMT−5 on 14 October 2026</strong>. The request originated from <code>45.138.12.188</code>, with ASN <code>218785 — TC DATACENTER LIMITED</code> shown in the dashboard.</p><p>The event was labelled <strong>Not mitigated</strong>. This describes the security disposition shown for that request; it does not prove that the requested file existed or that its contents were returned. The supplied record does not show a response body or HTTP status.</p><p>A separate pre-rule browser screenshot shows portfolio content at the same path. That is consistent with fallback routing, but does not establish the routing configuration or the original request's response. The IP and ASN are event context, not attribution to a person or proof that the network operator performed the request.</p></section>
      <section id="control"><h2>02 / Apply a targeted control</h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <code>JCF-Block-Git-Metadata</code><br /><strong>Action:</strong> <code>Block</code></p><pre><code>{rule}</code></pre><p>The expression lowercases the request path and matches the literal substring <code>/.git/</code>. It is path-based, not an IP block: requests from other addresses can also match.</p><p class="flow">Client request → Cloudflare custom rule → Matching path: Block<br />Nonmatching path → Continue through the remaining applicable rules and routing</p><p>The supplied rule editor shows this expression, Block selected and Active selected. A later rules-list screenshot shows the saved rule. Cloudflare documents custom rules and the Block action as available on Free; this implementation requires no paid feature. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/waf/custom-rules/">Cloudflare custom rules documentation</a>.</p></section>
      <section id="validation"><h2>03 / Validate without overstating the result</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Evidence and verification status</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Evidence boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Original request</td><td>Not mitigated</td><td>Visible in the expanded sampled log; no response status/body provided.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Saved custom rule</td><td>Block</td><td>Editor and subsequent rule-list captures reviewed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Subsequent browser test</td><td>Cloudflare block page</td><td>Visible screenshot; exact rule attribution requires a matching Security Event.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>HTTP and HTTPS tests</td><td>Both reported blocked by the site owner</td><td>The available block screenshot hides the scheme. HSTS or redirects can upgrade an HTTP browser attempt to HTTPS; separate raw-protocol results are not established.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Homepage and HSTS</td><td>HTTP 200; HSTS max-age=2592000</td><td>Independent HTTPS HEAD check on 14 October 2026, 23:30 GMT−5. The header did not include includeSubDomains or preload.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div><p>HSTS was already enabled by the owner. It concerns HTTPS enforcement by supporting clients; it does not replace the path-blocking rule.</p><h3>Reproducible follow-up</h3><p>For an authorized retest, record each request's timestamp, status, Location header and Ray ID; correlate the Ray ID with the rule in Cloudflare Security Events. Keep HTTP and HTTPS requests separate and disable automatic redirect following. Also check that the homepage and ordinary project pages remain accessible. Do not disable the existing rule to recreate the historical event.</p></section>
      <section id="evidence"><h2>04 / Evidence gallery</h2><p>Original screenshots supplied by the site owner. Publication copies are cropped to remove account details, browser chrome and unrelated traffic. Captions identify what each image proves; no reconstructed dashboard images are used.</p>
      <figure><a href="/labs/001/e01-request.png"><img src="/labs/001/e01-request.png" alt="Expanded Cloudflare request showing Not mitigated, GET /.git/config and the source ASN." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>E01 · Original request detail. Cropped to retain the relevant event and expanded details.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><a href="/labs/001/e02-rule.png"><img src="/labs/001/e02-rule.png" alt="Rule editor showing JCF-Block-Git-Metadata, the lowercase path expression, Block and Active." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>E02 · Rule editor before deployment; configuration intent, not standalone proof of deployment.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><a href="/labs/001/e03-saved.png"><img src="/labs/001/e03-saved.png" alt="Saved custom rule listed with the Block action." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>E03 · Subsequent saved rule listing. The displayed event counter is not used as a success metric.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><a href="/labs/001/e04-block.png"><img src="/labs/001/e04-block.png" alt="Cloudflare block page stating that access to jcfinfra.com is blocked." loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>E04 · Subsequent block page. Browser chrome and the Ray ID footer are excluded. Scheme, status code and exact rule attribution are not visible.</figcaption></figure>
      </section>
      <section id="limits"><h2>05 / Scope and lessons learned</h2><ul><li>This is a narrow control for a specific path pattern, not a complete WAF assessment or proof that the site is secure.</li><li>The exact expression does not match a bare <code>/.git</code> without the trailing slash. Encoded paths, normalization behaviour, alternate hostnames and direct-origin access were not tested.</li><li>Keep repository metadata out of published build artifacts. Edge filtering is an additional control, not a substitute for safe deployment contents.</li><li>Sampled logs are not a complete record of all traffic. Request source geography and ASN registration geography may differ; neither establishes an attacker's location.</li><li>Hosting: Cloudflare Pages, based on the owner's dashboard confirmation of <code>jcfinfra-website.pages.dev</code>, <code>jcfinfra.com</code> and <code>www.jcfinfra.com</code>. The zone rule should not be assumed to protect the separate pages.dev hostname.</li></ul><p><strong>Practical lesson:</strong> identify a specific exposure pattern, apply a proportionate control, validate observable behaviour and retain the limits of the evidence in the final report.</p></section>
    </div>
  </main>
